  "prompt_text": "This figure compares different solar concentrator tracking approaches by plotting concentration against tracking range. The vertical axis shows concentration on a logarithmic scale, while the horizontal axis shows tracking range in degrees. The main message is a tradeoff: systems with wider tracking or beam-steering capability generally extend to larger angles, while very high concentration is achieved by only some concepts and often with added complexity or immature components.\n\nThree broad groups are outlined. “Microtracking” concepts sit mostly at lower tracking angles but can reach moderate to high concentration. A smaller “reactive” subset includes ideas such as a microtracking waveguide and a moving-aperture cavity, clustered around roughly tens to low hundreds in concentration. A larger region toward higher angles contains beam-steering approaches, including rotating prisms, rotating mirrors, and a quad-prism optofluidic concept, which appear to span the widest tracking range and include some of the highest concentration values, approaching or exceeding 1,000x.\n\nOther intermediate designs, such as refractive-reflective systems and wide-angle lenses, occupy midrange positions. Color cues suggest orange labels indicate mechanical complexity, while red labels mark materials or components that may not yet be in production; an asterisk notes possible gaps in tracking coverage.",
